            <section id="service">
                <h2>2. What BillWatch does</h2>
                <p>BillWatch is designed to help users identify recurring bills, detect meaningful changes, understand the estimated monthly and annual impact of those changes, and, when supporting evidence is available, explain why a bill changed.</p>
                <p>BillWatch may use bank transaction history, account metadata, provider statements, extracted statement text, bill history, and other evidence you provide or authorize. The product is transaction-first: transactions may identify recurring bills, while statements and other evidence may help explain changes.</p>
                <p>BillWatch may change, improve, suspend, or discontinue features over time. A feature shown in testing, beta, documentation, or marketing may not be available to every user at all times.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="accounts">
                <h2>3. Accounts, credentials, and security</h2>
                <p>You are responsible for maintaining the confidentiality of your BillWatch credentials and for activity performed through your account. You must provide accurate account information and promptly update it when necessary.</p>
                <p>BillWatch may offer security features such as email verification, password recovery, authenticator-based two-factor authentication, recovery codes, and session protections. You are responsible for safeguarding any recovery codes and devices used to access your account.</p>
                <p>You must notify BillWatch through the support method made available in the service if you believe your account has been compromised.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="financial-data">
                <h2>4. Financial institution connections and Plaid</h2>
                <p>BillWatch may use Plaid or another authorized data provider to connect to supported financial institutions. When you start a connection, you may interact directly with that provider's connection flow and its own terms and privacy practices.</p>
                <p>By connecting an account, you authorize BillWatch and its data provider to retrieve the account, transaction, balance, institution, and related information you select and authorize for the purpose of providing BillWatch features.</p>
                <p>BillWatch is designed as a read-only monitoring product. BillWatch does not use connected financial data to initiate payments, transfers, trades, withdrawals, deposits, or other movement of funds.</p>
                <p>Financial institutions and data providers can change, delay, restrict, revoke, or stop data access. BillWatch is not responsible for the availability, accuracy, or timeliness of a third party's systems.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="statements">
                <h2>5. Statement uploads and document processing</h2>
                <p>You may be able to upload provider statements or other supported documents so BillWatch can extract bill information and compare it with transaction and bill history. You represent that you have the right to provide any document you upload.</p>
                <p>BillWatch may classify documents, extract text, run OCR, parse bill fields, validate candidate values, match a statement to a bill, compare prior history, detect changes, and create explanations or alerts. Some processing can fail or produce incomplete results because documents vary widely in quality and format.</p>
                <p>Do not upload documents unrelated to your own BillWatch use or documents containing information you are not authorized to provide.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="automation">
                <h2>6. Automated analysis and AI-assisted features</h2>
                <p>BillWatch uses deterministic software for core financial arithmetic, ownership checks, security decisions, historical comparisons, and other verifiable calculations. Where enabled, BillWatch may also use automated or AI-assisted tools to interpret limited evidence, such as identifying candidate reasons a bill changed.</p>
                <p>AI-assisted output is not treated as independent proof. BillWatch is designed to validate candidate facts against available evidence and may say that it does not know why a bill changed when the evidence is insufficient.</p>
                <p>Automated results can still be wrong. You should review important findings against your provider statement, financial institution records, or provider account before making financial decisions.</p>
            </section>

